{
  "id": "COMPOUND:36844 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Beta-Maaliene is proposed to interact with DNA polymerase iota through a series of molecular targets that are all implicated in the regulation of DNA replication. The evidence suggests that beta-Maaliene acts as a target for multiple compounds, all of which are known to interact with DNA polymerase iota. These compounds—10'(Z),13'(E),15'(E)-Heptadecatrienylhydroquinone, 2-Hydroxy-3-methoxystrychnine, and Trigonostemine E—each have a documented interaction with DNA polymerase iota, indicating a potential mechanism of action that involves direct binding or modulation of the enzyme. The presence of multiple distinct targets for beta-Maaliene, all leading to the same downstream effect, supports the hypothesis that beta-Maaliene may exert its biological activity through a shared mechanism that targets DNA polymerase iota. The integrated evidence suggests that beta-Maaliene could inhibit DNA polymerase iota, potentially disrupting DNA synthesis and leading to cell cycle arrest or apoptosis. The strength of the evidence is supported by the high scores in the paths, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction between beta-Maaliene and DNA polymerase iota.",
